{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Interpreted the follow-up receipt (CheckRun 478561f525b3471ca92b9aa20c6d12df), whose four comparisons all hold: while the node still owed the queued read request but was not leader, no read state was produced for it, and the demotion happened at the term it had led at, so the module's own quorum check ended the leadership rather than a peer's higher term (index 52); once that node led again at term 3 and committed at index 12, the stale request was released with read index 12 and the context it was accepted with (index 54, both comparisons). So the answer to the child question is that such a request is never cancelled, is not answered while the node is not leader, and is answered only if that node leads again and commits — then with the index reached at that later release, which is at least as new as the one recorded at acceptance, i.e. late but not stale, and nothing here is a defect. Recorded the observation as a Fact, wrote the child account (conclusion holds, defect_claim null), and revised the parent read-index account so the half that used to rest on source reading is now measured, dropping the limitation that said the loss path was unexercised and adding a joint history explaining how the two receipts relate. Remaining limits: quorum was exactly leader plus one follower, and the never-leads-again case stays unobservable beyond the source fact that no other code path can answer it. Validation passed with no execution requested.\"}"}}
